<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- ============================================================
	     PUSH NOTIFICATIONS

	     Without this key the app cannot register with APNs at all, and
	     PushNotifications.register() fails with "no valid aps-environment
	     entitlement". That is the whole reason reminders and message
	     notifications exist on this platform — see js/nativepush.js.

	     'development' is correct in the source file. Xcode signs debug
	     and TestFlight/App Store builds against different APNs
	     environments and the distribution profile promotes this value on
	     export; you do not maintain two copies of it.

	     ⚠️ BUT THE SERVER HAS TO BE TOLD WHICH ONE IT IS TALKING TO.
	     A build run from Xcode gets a SANDBOX device token; TestFlight
	     and the App Store get PRODUCTION ones, and sending one to the
	     other's host answers 400 BadDeviceToken — which is
	     indistinguishable from an uninstalled app, so the row is pruned
	     and the device silently stops receiving. Set the APNS_ENV
	     function secret to 'sandbox' while testing from Xcode and
	     'production' (or unset) for everything else. See
	     supabase/functions/_shared/apns.ts.
	     ============================================================ -->
	<!-- ============================================================
	     APP GROUP — the only channel between the web layer and the
	     home screen widget.

	     ⚠️ THE SAME STRING APPEARS IN FOUR PLACES: here,
	     SomedayWidget.entitlements, APP_GROUP in WidgetBridge.swift and
	     WIDGET_GROUP in SomedayWidget.swift. A mismatch throws nothing —
	     the app writes into a suite the widget does not read, and the
	     widget sits on its placeholder forever. It also has to be
	     enabled on BOTH App IDs in the developer portal.
	     ============================================================ -->
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>group.com.landonjames.somedaywelldie</string>
	</array>

	<!-- ============================================================
	     SIGN IN WITH APPLE

	     ⚠️ ALSO A PAID-PROGRAM CAPABILITY. Like Push and Associated
	     Domains, a Personal Team cannot provision it and the target
	     will not sign until the App ID has it enabled.

	     And Supabase has to be told about it separately —
	     Authentication → Providers → Apple, with a Services ID and a
	     key from the developer portal. Without that the button draws
	     and the sign-in answers "Unsupported provider". See
	     js/appleauth.js.
	     ============================================================ -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.applesignin</key>
	<array>
		<string>Default</string>
	</array>

	<key>aps-environment</key>
	<string>development</string>

	<!-- ============================================================
	     UNIVERSAL LINKS

	     ⚠️ REPLACE THE HOST BELOW WITH THE DOMAIN THE WEB APP IS
	     DEPLOYED AT, and make it the same one as APP_WEB_ORIGIN in
	     js/config.js. Three things have to name the same host — this,
	     that constant, and the apple-app-site-association file served by
	     it. If any of them disagrees, iOS declines to open the app and
	     the link goes to Safari, with no error anywhere.

	     Leave the entry as-is and links simply never match; the app
	     builds and runs exactly as it does today.

	     `applinks:` and nothing else. `webcredentials:` would opt into
	     shared password autofill, which is a different feature with its
	     own AASA section, and this app has no reason to claim it.
	     ============================================================

	     ENABLED 3 Sep 2026. The web app is served from
	     https://landonjames1.github.io/SomedayWellDie/ — a GitHub Pages
	     PROJECT site — so the app lives under a path while the AASA file
	     must be served from the DOMAIN ROOT. Those are two different
	     repos on GitHub Pages, and the root one is what has to exist:

	       landonjames1.github.io  ->  /.well-known/apple-app-site-association
	       SomedayWellDie          ->  the app itself, under /SomedayWellDie/

	     ⚠️ TWO THINGS STILL HAVE TO BE DONE BY HAND OR THIS SIGNS BUT
	     NEVER MATCHES, WITH NO ERROR ANYWHERE:
	       1. Enable "Associated Domains" on the App ID in the developer
	          portal. Without it the target FAILS TO SIGN.
	       2. Put the real 10-character Team ID into
	          .well-known/apple-app-site-association, and serve that file
	          from the domain root over https, with no redirect, as
	          application/json and behind no auth.
	     iOS fetches the file ONCE at install and caches it, so delete and
	     reinstall after changing it — nothing else refreshes it.
	     ============================================================ -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.associated-domains</key>
	<array>
		<string>applinks:landonjames1.github.io</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
